@model WorkshopModel
@{ ViewData["Title"] = "Order context"; }
<h1>Ask about this order.</h1><p class="lead">A context rule checks domain data. It is separate from the user making the request.</p>
<section class="card"><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/orders?preset=@Model.Persona.Preset&order=vip">VIP order</a><a class="button secondary" href="/orders?preset=@Model.Persona.Preset&order=standard">Standard order</a></div><dl><dt>Order.Id</dt><dd>@Model.Persona.Order.Id</dd><dt>Order.Vip</dt><dd>@Model.Persona.Order.Vip</dd><dt>ExpressCheckout</dt><dd>@(Model.Flags[FeatureFlags.Express] ? "ON" : "OFF")</dd></dl><pre>await features.IsEnabledAsync("ExpressCheckout", order)</pre><p>The sample registers <code>Order</code> once with key <code>Id</code> and mapped <code>Vip</code>/<code>Total</code> properties, then passes this instance per evaluation. The imported rule requires <code>Vip = true</code>.</p><p>Without an entity: <strong>@(Model.ExpressWithoutEntity ? "ON" : "OFF")</strong>. An entity-targeted feature fails closed when its entity is missing.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Combine user and entity rules deliberately</h2><p>User/web rules have their own Any/All setting. Entity rules use their separate Any/All setting, falling back to the user setting when omitted. When both groups exist, both groups must match.</p></section>
